# Frontend style conventions

Conventions for the frontend styling layer in the `front` source tree. This covers SCSS Modules, global styles boundaries, naming, responsive rules, and stylelint hygiene.

## Source of truth

- Treat **`front/.stylelintrc`** as the full source of truth for style linting rules.
- If this document and stylelint configuration disagree, **stylelint wins**.

## Style scope and structure

- Use **SCSS Modules** for component-local styles (`*.module.scss`) colocated with UI components.
- Keep global styles and shared Sass setup only in **`application/styles`**.
- Do not add global resets or global tag styling inside component module styles.

## Naming inside module styles

- Use one BEM-like pattern: **`block`**, **`&__element`**, **`&_modifier`**. Keep class tokens **lowerCamelCase**. The root **`block`** class should match the component filename stem.
- When nesting uses **`&`** shorthand and the **full** class token does not appear on the selector line, put **`// .block__element`** immediately above **`&__element`** (that comment line: token only, no blank line before the rule). **Omit** that comment when the selector line already contains **`.block__element`** literally. Do **not** add prose, parentheses, or cross-references on the token-only comment line; put any extra context on a **separate** comment line **above** it.
- When several **`&__element`** branches are **comma-grouped** into one rule block, put **one** token-only comment immediately above **each** branch’s **`&__…`** (same idea as the single-branch rule). **Example:** **`// .block__a`** / **`&__a,`** / **`// .block__b`** / **`&__b {`**. Do **not** put several full-class tokens in **one** comment line.

## Tokens and values

- Prefer shared design tokens from `application/styles/module` over ad-hoc literals.
- Prefer helper units from the module (e.g. **`rem()`**) for spacing and typography.
- Avoid hard-coded magic values when the same intent already exists in shared variables.
- Avoid pixel units where a relative/tokenized value can express the same intent.
- For fractional values, prefer decimal notation over equivalent percentages when both are valid.

## Responsive rules

- Keep a mobile-first baseline and scale layout via shared breakpoint variables.
- Use breakpoints from the shared vars layer instead of ad-hoc media values.
- Keep media-query strategy consistent within one module (avoid mixed, conflicting patterns in the same file).
- For pixel breakpoint edges, use the **`*.98`** convention so adjacent ranges do not overlap (e.g. **`max-width: 767.98px`**).

## Layout and spacing

- Prefer module classes and class composition over inline styles in JSX.
- Prefer layout spacing with **`gap`** in flex/grid containers instead of directional margins.
- If margins are unavoidable, prefer **trailing-edge** physical longhands over **leading-edge** ones.
- For centering in grid layouts, prefer **`place-items: center`**.
- Do **not** use **logical-axis** CSS (property names that use **`inline`** or **`block`** as an axis segment); prefer **physical** longhands. The **`inset`** shorthand alone is **allowed**.

## Nesting and compound selectors

- After **`&`**, do not keep **descendant** chains or other long compounds on **one line**; **split with nesting** under **`&`**. Prefer nested blocks over one long **`&`** chain when another class shares the selector line. When a nested selector **starts with a combinator**, omit a redundant leading **`&`**. For token-only comments, follow [Naming inside module styles](#naming-inside-module-styles). **One illustration:** **`+ .block__item`** nested under **`&__item`**, not **`& + &`** for the same entity.

## Third-party overrides

- Use `:global(...)` only when targeting third-party library markup.
- Use `!important` only for third-party overrides that cannot be resolved by specificity.
- When using either pattern above, add a short inline comment explaining why it is required.

## Stylelint hygiene

- Run the package stylelint script after edits; committed code must pass with zero warnings if CI enforces it.
